    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes -\n    EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute Weisfeiler-Lehman hashes of every node's neighborhood, round by\n    round.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Compute Weisfeiler-Lehman hashes of every node's neighborhood, round by\n    round.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n  .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Compute Weisfeiler-Lehman hashes of every node's neighborhood, round by round.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute Weisfeiler-Lehman hashes of every node's neighborhood, round by round.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L22952-L23085){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes(\n    node_attr=None,\n    edge_attr=None,\n    iterations=3,\n    digest_size=16,\n    include_initial_labels=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Weisfeiler-Lehman subgraph hashes](../assets/graphs/weisfeiler-lehman-subgraph-hashes.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A path of five nodes, A to E, colored by their second-round hashes: the two ends A and E share one hash (accent), the inner nodes B and D share another (teal), and the middle node C has its own (violet). The card lists the first six hex digits of each class in matching colors: 68f978 for A and E, b51b1d for B and D, and 0ea5aa for C.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nFor each node, the k-th hash summarizes the structure within k hops of the node (its\n\"rooted subtree\" after k rounds of Weisfeiler-Lehman relabeling; see\n`weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash()`). Two nodes with equal hashes at round k look alike up\nto that depth, so the hashes can be used to find structurally equivalent nodes, to\ncompare neighborhoods across different graphs (hashes are comparable between graphs\nhashed with the same settings), or as node features in machine learning, as in\ngraph2vec. The values are identical to NetworkX's `weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes`\n(NetworkX 3.5 or later), except on directed graphs with `edge_attr=`, where neighbor\nlabels keep their direction prefix (see `weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash()`).\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node attribute used as the initial label. Every node must have it.\n\n<code>[edge_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional edge attribute whose value prefixes neighbor labels. Every edge must have it.\n\n<code>[iterations]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[3]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Number of rounds (default 3).\n\n<code>[digest_size]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[16]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Size of the BLAKE2b digests, in bytes. Default 16.\n\n<code>[include_initial_labels]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, also include the hash of the initial label as the first entry of every\n    list.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, list[str]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   For every node ID, its list of hashes, one per round (from the nearest\n    neighborhood to the widest).\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `iterations` is not positive, or a node or edge lacks a named attribute.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nEach round replaces a node's label by the BLAKE2b hash of its label followed by the\nsorted labels of its neighbors, and appends the new label to the node's list. With\ndegree labels the first entry is the hash of the degree, matching NetworkX. Each round\ncosts $O(E \\log \\Delta)$.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash`) \n\n:   One hash for the whole graph.\n\n[](`~automorphism_cosets`) \n\n:   Which nodes are interchangeable, exactly.\n\n[](`~simrank_similarity`) \n\n:   Similarity of nodes by the similarity of their neighbors.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a path of five nodes, the two ends look alike, as do the two inner neighbors, and the\nmiddle node is unique. The second-round hashes show those three classes:\n\n::: {#3042af30 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npath = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\")])\n\nhashes = path.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes(iterations=2)\nsecond = {path.get_label_from_id(n): h[1][:6] for n, h in hashes.items()}\nsecond\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'C': '0ea5aa', 'A': '68f978', 'E': '68f978', 'D': 'b51b1d', 'B': 'b51b1d'}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Atoms that look alike\n\nEach hash describes an atom's surroundings out to one more bond than the last. In\ncaffeine, by element, the first round leaves 9 classes among the 14 atoms: the three\nmethyl carbons C1, C7 and C8 look alike, as do the nitrogens they are bonded to and the\ntwo oxygens. Each of those nitrogens is bonded to two more carbons, so the methyl carbons\nstill agree in the second round. The third round sees what those carbons are bonded to,\nand tells all 14 atoms apart:\n\n::: {#994aac3e .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncaffeine = ec.molecules()[\"caffeine\"]\nhashes = caffeine.weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes(node_attr=\"element\")\n\n[len({h[i] for h in hashes.values()}) for i in range(3)]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[9, 12, 14]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe atoms that share a second-round hash with another atom are the three methyl carbons:\n\n::: {#9d830e0f .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nsecond = [h[1] for h in hashes.values()]\nalike = [node for node, h in hashes.items() if second.count(h[1]) > 1]\n\ncaffeine.plot(highlight=alike, label_by=\"label\", seed=1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"5.6 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"5.6\" y=\"14.0\" 